## Reticular synthesis of HKUST-like tbo MOFs with enhanced $\mathsf{\pmb{C}}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ storage
ǀDepartment of Chemistry, University of Crete, Voutes 71003 Heraklion, Greece; ǀDepartment of Materials Science and Technology, University of Crete, Voutes, 71003 Heraklion, Greece; ‡Functional Materials Design, Discovery & Development (FMD3), Advanced Membranes & Porous Materials Center, Division of Physical Sciences and Engineering, 4700 King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST), Thuwal 23955-6900, Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.
KEYWORDS. Supermolecular Building Layers, HKUST, tbo, Reticular chemistry, Methane storage.
## INTRODUCTION
The utilization of fossil fuels in the energy production and the mobile transportation sector results in the emission of massive amounts of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the atmosphere, instigating numerous health and environmental issues.1 Thus, prompt deployment of environmental friendly fuels is of utmost importance. Natural gas, consisting of nearly $95\%\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ is a good candidate for replacing gasoline in mobile transportation and coal in stationary power plants, since it can sustain the same energy demand with amply lessened release of CO, $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{\mathrm{x}},$ $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ pollutants.2 Nevertheless, the deployment of methane as a typical fuel in the automotive industry has been hindered by practicalities associated to cost-effective and efficient storage of methane at concrete temperatures and pressures.3
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) represent an exceptional class of functional solid-state materials suitable for numerous applications, due to their unique modularity, unprecedented tunability, and high degree of porosity combined with diverse framework functionalities.4 Accordingly, MOF chemistry offers great potential to address challenging issues pertaining to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.3b, 5 As an illustration of a MOF compound with high promise for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage, HKUST- $\cdot1^{6}$ was reported to be the best performing material at intermediate pressures $(35^{-65}$ bar), showing a total volumetric uptake of $\scriptstyle267\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $_{298\mathrm{K}}$ and $65$ bar with a corresponding working capacity $(5-65$ bar) of 190 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ .7 Although HKUST-1 exhibits remarkable performance in comparison to the best storage media, it is still far below the gravimetric and volumetric DOE target of $\mathbf{0.5\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{g}}$ and $264~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}/\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ in the range of temperatures between $-40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , typical for vehicle in real operation.3b, 8
Successful implementation of reticular chemistry and the molecular building block approach offer nowadays potential to ideally target MOFs with desired and appropriate structural features. For example, HKUST- $\cdot\mathbf{1}^{9}$ material that has an underlying tbo-a topology, can be further interpreted and regarded as constructed from a 2- periodic arrays of squares, each consistent with an augmented 4,4 square lattice (sql-a), that are cross-linked through 4-connected pillars containing copper paddle wheels. This underlying layer, isolated through deconstruction of the crystal structure, can be targeted and constructed from judiciously selected metals and ligands. Then, rational design using pillaring strategy, also called Supermolecular Building Layer (SBL) approach,10 can be readily implemented using appropriately selected organic pillars that permit to generate the anticipated $^3$ -periodic MOF. Based on this background and rationally, it is conceivable to reasonably target/synthesize an expanded or/and functionalized HKUST-l like tbo-MOF with higher gravimetric and volumetric surface area which in turn will result in a relatively higher $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake.
The SBL approach has first been introduced and employed by Eddaoudi et al. to construct a new family of isoreticular MOFs with tbo topology by using quadrangular (octatopic) organic ligands instead of trigonal (tritopic) ones as in the case of HKUST-1.10 The use of quadrangular organic ligands offers the advantages to modulate porosity and functionalization of the resulting material but most importantly, it prevents self-interpenetration as it is observed in the case of MOF-388.11 In this way it is possible to efficiently utilize extended organic ligands and synthesize tailor made tbo-MOF materials with specific pore size and volume. So far, there are only few examples of tbo-MOFs fabricated from quadrangular organic ligands,12 and studied for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.
$4$ -connected node. c) tbo-a topology. d) Crystal structure of Cu-tboMOF- $5$ showing two types of cavities.
The first attempt to fabricate high surface area HKUST-like tbo-MOF was achieved using tetraisophthalate ligand connected through methoxy groups to a $^4$ -connected benzene core10, 13 (see scheme S2). Although the surface area and pore volume were improved, the strategy of extending the pillars without introduction of functional groups led unfortunately to a decrease in the total and working $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gravimetric and volumetric uptake in the $35^{-65}$ bar range as this strategy induces the removal of dimeric paddlewheel units carrying strong $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption sites (see scheme $S_{3}$ ).13 Therefore, maintaining adequate high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ interaction sites associated with the surface area and pore volume increase is not straightforward particular in case of tbo-MOF platform. Accordingly, it was compelling for us to explore the use of SBL strategy to develop novel HKUST-1 like tbo-MOFs with extended linkers having strong $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ interaction sites.
To address this challenge, it is important to note that tbo based structure requires an isophthalate-based octatopic organic linker (four isophthalate groups) with specific geometrical constrains which limits the choice of suitable organic linkers.12b To overcome these limitations, two different strategies could provide a suitable pathway to construct the targeted MOF: (i) the use of flexible octatopic linkers that has been proven to be a successful strategy10 but in turn jeopardizes the framework stability due to the increased strain energy of the ligand12b (ii) the employment of highly aromatic, rigid octatopic organic pillars that can maintain/enhance the stability of the material and also increase $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake through favorable $\pi...C\mathrm{H}_{4}$ interactions. Moreover, these interactions could be further enhanced by functionalizing the aromatic rings with methyl groups. It is evident that this strategy is highly challenging and implies the design and synthesis of new, highly aromatic rigid octatopic linkers suitable for the formation of expanded tbo-MOFs.
Indeed, we designed and synthesized the rigid octatopic organic linker $^{3,6}$ -dimetheyl- $^{\cdot1,2,4,5}$ -tetra(biphenyl- $^{\cdot3^{'},5^{'}}$ -dicarboxylic acid)benzene $\mathbf{(H_{8}L)}$ shown in figure 1, which upon solvothermal reaction in presence of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ readily afforded the desired tboMOFs. We reasoned that the presence of two methyl groups attached to the central phenyl ring is beneficial because it will i) increase the electron density of this ring which will induce the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -framework interaction enhancement at this site and in turn, will augment the total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity and ii) will reduce the relatively large size of the central structure cavity, keeping in this way the overall porosity at the micropore region.
Here we show that while the Co-tbo-MOF- $5$ and Zn-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ MOFs were found unstable upon solvent removal, due to the resultant incompatible coordination geometry of the metal center, the Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ showed excellent structural stability and methane sorption capabilities. Notably, in comparison with HKUST-1, the present expanded Cu-tbo-MOF- $5$ analogue displays a $115\%$ and $47\%$ increase in the gravimetric and volumetric surface area, respectively.14 Detailed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption measurements revealed that Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ exhibits very high gravimetric and volumetric storage capacities reaching $372\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $(\mathbf{o.266\g\g^{-1}})$ and $221~\mathrm{{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ respectively, at $^{85}$ bar and 298 K. The gravimetric working capacity between $5^{-80}$ bar at $_{298\mathrm{~K~}}$ is $\mathsf{294~c m^{3}}$ (STP) $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $\left(0.217\mathrm{~g~g~}^{-1}\right)$ ). This value surpasses two of the currently best performing MOFs for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage, HKUST- $\cdot\mathbf{1}^{7}$ $\left(\mathbf{0.162\g\g^{-1}}\right)$ and ${\bf M O F-519}^{6}$ (0.172 $\textbf{g}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) and places Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ among the best performing MOFs for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage. Quantum chemistry calculations and Monte Carlo simulations confirmed the experimental results and shed light on $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -framework interactions that helped to better understand the observed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption mechanism.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The design of new, large and rigid quadrangular isophthalate-based organic linkers for the construction of expanded tbo-MOFs necessitates to define the appropriate geometrical attribute to readily pillar the 2-periodic layers. The in-deep analysis of the structure reveals that the distance between the neighboring isophthalate groups of the organic moieties, indicated in figure 1b by the red arrow, is of prime importance for the fabrication of the targeted MOF. Indeed, as the distance between the carboxylates belonging to an isophthalic moiety is almost fixed, ${\sim}6$ Å, the required distance between two neighboring isophthalates has to be of ${\sim}9\mathrm{~\AA~}$ to accommodate the distance between two paddle-wheels from the 2-peridic layers. The use of such an organic linker with the adequate distance and consequently with the appropriate conformation will allow the generation of the desired tbo-MOF with no conformational change of the ligand and will in-fine result in relatively more stable structure.12b The successful synthesis of the designed ligand, as shown in figure 2, allowed us to fabricate the desired tbo-MOFs. Indeed, solvothermal reaction between $\mathbf{H_{8}L}$ and $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}}\mathrm{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ , $C_{\mathsf{U}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{3})_{2}{\mathrm{:}}3{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ or $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}\cdot6H_{2}O}$ readily afforded the construction of the corresponding MOFs with the expected tbo topology. These MOFs are denoted here as Co-tbo-MOF- $5$ , $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ and Zntbo-MOF- $5$ respectively. Powder X-ray diffraction analysis (PXRD) indicated that the three MOFs are isostructural compounds (see figure $S_{7}$ ).
Figure 2. a) The structure of $\mathbf{H_{8}L}$ linker a) as predicted by DFT, b) in Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ and c) in $Z\mathrm{n}$ -tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ . d) Overlay of the DFTpredicted structure of the ligand and that found in Cu-tbo-MOF-5, showing that there is only a small change in the dihedral angle of the isophthalate groups, when $\mathbf{L}^{8-}$ is incorporated into the tbo framework.
Initially, single crystals were isolated only in the case of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ (colorless plates, see figure $S{5}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ) while Co-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ were isolated in a microcrystalline form. Remarkably, green plate-like single crystals of $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ were obtained in a high quality through a facile, single-crystal-to-single-crystal topotactic and quantitative exchange of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ (see SI). The quantitative replacement of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ was confirmed by EDS (see figure S6). The structure of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -tbo$\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ and Cu-tbo-MOF- $5$ was determined by single crystal X-ray diffraction experiments (see Table S1 and S2). The purity of these materials have been confirmed by comparison between the experimental and calculated PXRD patterns (see figure $s_{7}$ ). Given that both tbo MOFs are isostructural, the structure of $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ will be presented in more detail below.
Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ crystallizes in the orthorhombic system, space group $F_{\mathrm{{mmm}}}$ with large unit cell parameters, $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{a}=\overset{\cdot}{2}5.61\bar{6}(2)\ \bar{\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}},}\end{array}$ , $\mathbf{b}=28.157(3)~\AA$ Å and $\mathsf{c}=40.549\AA\overset{\circ}{4})\mathrm{~\AA~}$ resulting in a unit cell volume of $29247(5)\mathrm{\AA}^{3}$ . The corresponding volume of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ was found slightly bigger, reaching $_{29811}({\bf\lambda}_{10}){\mathrm{\bf\lambda}}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}{}^{3}$ , as expected due to the slightly longer $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ bond length. As expected from the tbo-type structure, the framework consists of dimeric paddlewheel $\mathrm{[M_{2}(O_{2}C-)_{4}}$ , $\l{\l}{\mathrm{~M~}}=\l{\mathrm{~Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ) units in which the metal cations exhibit a square pyramidal $\mathrm{MO}_{5}$ geometry (see figure 1). These $^4$ -connected paddlewheels are linked to each other’s through isophthalic moieties of the ligand, resulting in a neutral $^3$ -periodic framework with microporous cavities of different sizes (see figure 1). Precisely, the larger cavity of approximately $16\textup{\AA}$ in length along the c-axis is located between the layers (see orange sphere in figure 1) which has two different widths along the a-axis due to the different arrangement of the central phenyl ring carrying the two methyl groups, pointing alternatively inside and outside of the cavity. A smaller cavity of approximately 11 Å exists within the layers (see green sphere in figure 1).15 The size of these cavities was further confirmed experimentally by accurate microporous analysis in the case of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -tbo-MOF- $^{.5}$ using Ar at $87~\mathrm{K}$ . The total solvent accessible free volume for Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ was found to be $73\%$ , using the PLATON software.16
Proper activation of the Cu-tbo-MOF- $5$ was successfully achieved  by applying a straightforward activation procedure using volatile solvents such as acetone and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ to exchange the DMF molecules followed by drying under vacuum and heating at ${{60}^{\circ}}\mathrm{~C~}$ overnight (see SI). In contrast, all attempts to properly activate Co-tboMOF- $^{\cdot5}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ using available methodologies such as supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ drying failed and leading to a systematic structural collapse. The origin of such phenomena can be directly correlated to the unstable distorted square planar $\mathrm{MO}_{4}$ geometry $(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}^{2+},\mathrm{Zn}^{2+})$ resulted from the paddle-wheels de-solvation under activation process. Accordingly, upon activation, the coordinated solvent molecules located at the apical positions of the paddle-wheels are removed resulting in four coordinated metal centers. The formation of stable paddle-wheel units SBUs necessitates that metal centers adopt a square planar geometry. Although for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ the $^4$ -connected square planar environment is a stable geometry with carboxylatebased ligands, it is not the case for ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ cations which both prefer/adopt the more stable tetrahedral geometry.12f The later destroys the dimeric paddle-wheels leading to the collapse of the framework.12c
Argon adsorption isotherm recorded at $87\mathrm{K}$ confirmed the permanent porosity of $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ showing a fully reversible type-I isotherm, characteristic of a microporous material, see figure $^3$ . The BET (Langmuir) surface area, calculated in the pressure range 0.007-0.01 $\mathsf{p}/\mathsf{p}_{\mathrm{{o};\mathsf{\Lambda}}}$ , was found to be 3971 $\mathbf{m}^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $(4^{123}\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , which is more than double $(\mathbf{u}5\ \%)$ ) compared to the prototypical tbo MOF, HKUST- $\mathbf{\cdot1}$ (ΒΕΤ area, $\mathbf{1850\m^{2}\ g^{-1}})^{7}$ and $37\%$ higher than tbo-MOF- $^{2}$ .10, 13 Taking into account the crystallographic density of Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ $(\rho=\mathbf{o.595~g~cm^{-3}})$ , the calculated volumetric BET area is $2363~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ which is significantly higher $(47\ \%)$ compared to the corresponding value of HKUST- $\mathbf{\cdot1}$ $\left(\rho\:=\:0.868\mathrm{\textrm{gcm}^{-3}}\right.$ , $1606\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~cm}^{-3},$ ). The calculated geometric surface area using the nonorthoSA software17 (probe diameter $_{3.4\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ ) is $3850\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}^{2}\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}}$ which is very close to the experimental BET value and confirms that $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ framework retains its
structural integrity  upon pore system activation. This is also supported by the total pore volume calculated from the Ar adsorption isotherm at 0.992 $\mathsf{p}/\mathsf{p}_{\mathrm{{o}}}$ that it is found to be 1.12 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , which is in close agreement with the crystallographic value of $\mathbf{1.22\cm^{3}\ g^{-1}}$ . In addition to gassorption results, the structural integrity of activated Cutbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ was also confirmed by PXRD measurements (see figure S8). The pore size distribution was calculated using Non-Local Density Functional Theory (NLDFT) after a successful fitting of the Ar adsorption isotherm data using a suitable NLDFT kernel (see figure S11). Accordingly, the corresponding curve (see inset in figure 3) shows two distinct peaks centered at $16.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $11.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , in full agreement with the crystallographic analysis. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and variable temperature X-ray powder diffraction (VT-PXRD) measurements demonstrate that $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo$\mathrm{\bfMOF-}5$ is stable up to ${}^{250}{}^{\mathrm{{^{o}C}}}$ (see figures S26 and $S_{27}$ ).
The high gravimetric and volumetric surface area, due to the large pore volume in the micropore region, in combination with the high degree of aromaticity, the methyl groups at the central ring and the presence of unsaturated ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites, make $\mathtt{C u}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ a very promising material for gas storage. Accordingly, extensive $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sorption measurements at different temperatures and pressures were performed in order to evaluate the Cutbo-MOF- $5$ in terms of key $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ properties including the saturation uptake, total uptake, working capacity and isosteric heat of adsorption. $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption measurements recorded at its boiling point $(\mathbf{112~K})$ and up to 1 bar showed a fully reversible type-I isotherm, with a high saturation uptake of $691\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathbf{g}^{-1}=\mathbf{\frac{\pi}{2}}\mathbf{\pi}.$ , $\left(404\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{(STP)\thinspacecm^{-3}}\right)$ , see Figure S12. The total pore volume calculated at 0.99 $\mathsf{p}/\mathsf{p}_{\mathrm{{o}}}$ using $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ as a probe molecule is $\mathbf{1.17\cm^{3}\ g^{-1}}$ and is in an excellent agreement with both the experimental value obtained from Ar adsorption $\left(\mathbf{1.2\cm^{3}\ g}^{-1}\right)$ at $87~\mathrm{K}$ and the calculated from the crystal structure $\left(\mathbf{1.22{\cm}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{-1\upgamma}}\right)$ , indicating that the pore space in $\mathrm{Cu}$ -tbo-MOF- $5$ is fully accessible by $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules. From the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms recorded at $273\mathrm{K},283\mathrm{K}$ and 298 K, the calculated isosteric heat of adsorption at zero coverage, ${{\mathrm{{Q}}_{\mathrm{{st}}}}^{\mathrm{{\mathrm{{o}}}}}}$ , using a virial type equation was estimated to be $20.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ and remained nearly constant as function of the surface coverage (see figure $S_{15}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ). Interestingly, this value is higher compared to HKUST-1 $(\mathbf{1}7\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ and among the highest reported for MOFs (see Table S1).5a For example, Ni-MOF74 with very high density of open metal sites showed a ${{\mathrm{~\cal{Q}}}_{s t}}^{\mathrm{~\tiny{~o~}~}}$ value
of 21.4 kJ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . As we described in detail below, accurate theoretical calculations revealed that in Cu-tbo-MOF-5, in addition to the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites, there are three (3) distinct sites with interaction strength equal to that of open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites $\left({\sim}20\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ and reasoning the observed high ${{\mathrm{{Q}}_{s t}}^{o}}$ value.
A detailed comparison of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage properties in terms of working capacities between HKUST-1 and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -tboMOF-5 revealed important findings. In fact, Cu-tbo-MOF-5 shows higher gravimetric working capacity between 5-65 bar (more relevant to real applications than the 5-35 bar range due to the upper limit achievable with inexpensive two-stage compressors) by $22.7\%$ at 298K while in the range 5-80 bar the corresponding increase is $29.6\%$ . The volumetric working capacity shows a decrease of $16.8\%$ in the range 5-65 bar but a much lower decrease, $12.5\%$ , in the range 5-80 bar, both at 298K. Because a temperature decrease has a similar effect in $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake as the increase in pressure, it is relevant to compare the corresponding $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ working capacities between the two solids at a lower temperature. Remarkably, at 273K the volumetric working capacity in the range 5-65 bar is increased by $10.3\%$ (the percentage different between 298K and 273K is $27.1\%$ ) while the gravimetric working capacity shows an impressive increase of $60.1\%$ . These results are shown in figure 5c.
A very rare behavior is noticed in Cu-tbo-MOF-5. Indeed, the increase of both gravimetric and volumetric working capacity is observed as the temperature is decreasing, which is the opposite for HKUST-1 and other high performance MOFs including UTSA-76 and NU-111. This kind of dependence of the working capacity suggests that a combination of pressure and temperature swing could be an effective method for methane storage and release.19 For example, using Cu-tbo-MOF-5, an adsorption at $258~\mathrm{K}$ at 65 bar followed by a desorption at 5 bar and 298K, will result in a volumetric working capacity of $220\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ which is $83\%$ of the DOE target $(264~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , if the packing loss is ignored.
The above results could originate from the synergistic effect between pore volume (this correlates with surface area) and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -framwork interactions. Accordingly, due to higher pore volume and moderate $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -framework interactions in Cu-tbo-MOF-5, the uptake is smaller in the low pressure region (5 bar range) compared to the high pressure range (see figure 4), leading in this way to higher working capacities. The opposite is true for HKUST-1 where pore confinement due to smaller pore volume results in increased $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake at lower pressures and saturation close to 65 bar (see figure S24).
Computational methods were employed with the aim to identify the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption sites in Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ and in particular, to better understand the effect of the various structural features of the extended tbo-type framework on the observed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage properties. Accordingly, Quantum chemistry calculations and Monte Carlo simulations with the Grand Canonical ensemble (GCMC) were performed in order to investigate the energetics of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption and to simulate adsorption isotherms at different conditions. Interestingly, the calculations showed that Cu-tbo-MOF- $^{\cdot5}$ possesses four (4) highly energetic $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption sites of equal strength $\left({\sim}20\mathrm{~kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ ), indicated as I, II, III, and IV in figure 6 and one more, $\mathbf{V}_{:}$ , with relatively lower interaction energy (15 $\mathsf{k J\ m o l^{-1}},$ ). More specifically, in addition to the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites (II), usually being energetically the highest interaction sites in MOFs, there are three more, I, III and IV, of equal strength, located at the positions shown in figure 6. As expected from the tbo topology, I, II and III are the most energetic ones, similarly to HKUST-1.20 However, it is interesting to highlight that sites IV and $\mathbf{V}$ are also found to be highly energetic because of the particular structure of the ligand. The high adsorption strength of the IV site is attributed to the local geometry of the small v-shaped pocket of the ligand and the subsequent increased dispersion interactions of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecule $(\pi^{\cdots}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}$ interactions) with the opposite aromatic rings (see figure 6). A less energetic, but important in terms of its strength $({\bf-15}\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , is site $\mathbf{V}$ at the top of the central phenyl ring of the ligand carrying the two methyl groups. The calculations showed that the origin of the amplified strength of this site, as compared to the other phenyl rings of the ligand $\left({\bf-10~k J~m o l^{-1}},\right.$ ) , is the electron
